\[
s = \Omega \left( \frac{n \log k}{\max \{ \log(n/k), 1\} } \right).
\]
Since, by definition of $s$, at least half of all secret codes in $[k]^n$ can only be identified by the strategy $S$ after at least $s$ guesses, it follows that the expected number of queries needed to identify a uniformly chosen secret code is at least $s/2$.

